# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# .gitignore — PAFA-DoH Reproducibility Artifact
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# Companion to:
#   README.md                       (File 1 of 74)
#   REPRODUCIBILITY_CHECKLIST.md    (File 2 of 74)
#   LICENSE                         (File 3 of 74)
#   requirements.txt                (File 4 of 74)
#   environment.yml                 (File 5 of 74)
#   Dockerfile                      (File 6 of 74)  — contains the
#                                                      recommended
#                                                      .dockerignore
#                                                      that mirrors
#                                                      this file
#   setup.py                        (File 7 of 74)
#
# Thesis chapter: Chapter 4 (PAFA-DoH)
# Citation DOI:   10.1016/j.neunet.2025.108343   (Neural Networks, April 2026)
# License:        MIT (this file, as part of the artifact's source code)
# Artifact version: 1.0.0
#
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Purpose
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Keep the committed tree small, deterministic and reviewable. After a
# clean clone, `git status` must show exactly the tracked files listed
# in the "What is intentionally tracked" footer at the bottom of this
# file, and nothing else. Every rule below removes a class of files that
# are either (a) generated by the artifact at run time, (b) generated by
# Python tooling, or (c) local-only files that a reviewer or maintainer
# keeps in their working copy.
#
# This is *not* a `.dockerignore`. The recommended `.dockerignore` is
# shown in the header comment of the Dockerfile (File 6) and mirrors
# the relevant rules below. If you add a new rule here, consider whether
# the same rule should be added there, because the Docker build context
# is a strict subset of the git working tree that the artifact actually
# needs.
#
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# What is deliberately NOT ignored (see the footer at the bottom)
# ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The two header-only toy CSVs (Files 10h–10i), the CHECKSUMS manifest
# (File 10j), the `.gitkeep` placeholders inside `data/raw/` and
# `data/processed/` (Files 10l–10m), every `__init__.py` (Files 11a–16a,
# including the empty ones), and the eight YAML configs (Files 9–9h) are
# intentionally tracked. The footer restates this so that a future
# maintainer does not "helpfully" add a blanket `data/` or `configs/`
# rule and break the toy demo.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════


# ─── 1. Python bytecode and test caches ─────────────────────────────────────
# Every `python -m pytest`, `python script.py` and `pip install -e .` will
# generate bytecode and cache files. None of them are reproducible inputs;
# all are noise in the review diff.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage.xml
.cache/
.cache_*/
.tox/
.nox/


# ─── 2. Virtual environments ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Local-only. The reproducible environment is defined by requirements.txt
# (File 4) and environment.yml (File 5); a committed `.venv/` would defeat
# the purpose of having pins in the first place.
.venv/
venv/
env/
ENV/
.conda/
.python-version
*.conda


# ─── 3. Build and packaging artifacts ──────────────────────────────────────
# This artifact is installed with `pip install -e .` (dev install) or
# built as a wheel by CI (Docker builder stage, File 6). The intermediate
# artifacts are not part of the source and are regenerated deterministically
# from setup.py (File 7) plus requirements.txt.
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/
*.egg
.eggs/
pip-wheel-metadata/
*.whl
*.tar.gz
MANIFEST


# ─── 4. IDE and OS files ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Every IDE and OS in use by maintainers and reviewers; none of them are
# part of the artifact.
.idea/
.vscode/
*.iml
*.ipr
*.iws
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
*.lnk


# ─── 5. Jupyter and Colab caches ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reviewers frequently open the repo in Colab or JupyterLab (see
# REPRODUCIBILITY_CHECKLIST.md §2, "Google Colab" rows). Notebook outputs
# and checkpoints must not enter the review diff. If a notebook is
# intended to be part of the artifact, it is added as a tracked file and
# output-stripped before commit; no notebook is currently tracked in the
# 74-file plan.
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.ipynb
*_checkpoint.ipynb


# ─── 6. Artifact runtime outputs ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `outputs/` is where every script under scripts/ writes its results. The
# five sub-trees that land here are:
#
#   outputs/tables/       — JSON outputs of scripts/reproduce_tables/ (18a–18f)
#   outputs/figures/      — PDF/PNG outputs of scripts/reproduce_figures/ (19a–19d)
#   outputs/logs/         — JSON + human-readable logs from src/utils/logger.py (11d)
#   outputs/checkpoints/  — federated and SNN checkpoints from scripts/train_pafa.py (17a)
#   outputs/hardware_sim/ — simulator traces from src/hardware_simulation/ (16b–16c)
#
# The verification script (expected_outputs/verification_script.py, File 20i)
# compares freshly generated outputs against the committed expected files
# in expected_outputs/. The committed expected files (Files 20a–20h) are
# the reference; the generated outputs are not.
outputs/


# ─── 7. Downloaded datasets (re-fetchable from pinned DOI) ─────────────────
# The 32 GB Zenodo corpus (DOI 10.5281/zenodo.5957677) and the 2.5 GB
# CIC-DoH2023 bundle are downloaded at run time by data/download_datasets.py
# (File 10b) and are never committed. The two placeholder files
# `data/raw/.gitkeep` and `data/processed/.gitkeep` (Files 10l–10m) are
# explicitly un-ignored so that a clean clone preserves the empty
# directory structure that the download script writes into.
#
# The `!` exceptions MUST come *after* the ignore rule for their parent
# path, per gitignore spec §"Pattern Format" — otherwise the exception
# is unreachable.
data/raw/*
!data/raw/.gitkeep

data/processed/*
!data/processed/.gitkeep


# ─── 8. Toy-dataset generated rows (committed files stay tracked) ──────────
# The two tracked files (Files 10h–10i) are header-only: they contain the
# 47-column schema and zero data rows. When a user runs
# `python data/make_toy_dataset.py --mode synthetic --seed 42`, the
# generated rows are written to `data/processed/toy_dataset/` (already
# ignored via rule 7), NOT to the tracked `data/toy_dataset/` directory.
#
# This rule is a defensive net: if someone changes the generator to write
# in-place, the generated rows must NOT enter the diff. The tracked
# header-only files are matched exactly by name and un-ignored, so an
# accidental in-place generation would show up as a *modified* tracked
# file (which is what we want: a reviewer sees it in the diff and can
# reject it), while a *new* CSV in the same directory is ignored.
data/toy_dataset/*.csv
!data/toy_dataset/benign_sample.csv
!data/toy_dataset/malicious_sample.csv

# Any other stray CSV that a user might drop into data/ (e.g. a partial
# Zenodo extract) is ignored. The two pinned datasets have their own
# download paths under data/raw/ and data/processed/ (rule 7).
data/*.csv
data/*.parquet
data/*.feather
data/*.h5
data/*.hdf5
data/*.npy
data/*.npz


# ─── 9. Logs, environment files and local overrides ────────────────────────
# `.env` files often hold dataset DOIs or download tokens. They must never
# be committed. The `.env.example` template, if added later, is the only
# file matching `.env*` that should be tracked; un-ignore it explicitly.
*.log
logs/
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Local overrides of the pinned YAML configs. The eight committed configs
# (Files 9–9h) are the source of truth. A maintainer who needs to change
# a pin for a local experiment should copy the file to
# `configs/<name>.local.yaml` and modify the local copy; the `.local.yaml`
# suffix is ignored so the source config is never accidentally edited.
configs/*.local.yaml
configs/*.local.yml


# ─── 10. Temporary and backup files ────────────────────────────────────────
*.tmp
*.temp
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
*.patch
*.diff
*_backup/
*.old
*.swp


# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# What is intentionally tracked
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# After a clean clone, `git status` must show exactly the following
# categories of files, and nothing else:
#
#   1.  Top-level documentation and packaging metadata (Files 1–8):
#           README.md
#           REPRODUCIBILITY_CHECKLIST.md
#           LICENSE
#           requirements.txt
#           environment.yml
#           Dockerfile
#           setup.py
#           .gitignore  (this file)
#
#   2.  The eight YAML configs (Files 9–9h):
#           configs/default_pafa_config.yaml
#           configs/fl_dp_params.yaml
#           configs/snn_hyperparameters.yaml
#           configs/tda_params.yaml
#           configs/zk_attestation_params.yaml
#           configs/homomorphic_params.yaml
#           configs/baselines_config.yaml
#           configs/hardware_emulation_config.yaml
#
#   3.  The data-layer scripts (Files 10a–10g, 10j–10k):
#           data/data_utils.py
#           data/download_datasets.py
#           data/preprocess_doh2023.py
#           data/preprocess_zenodo.py
#           data/split_data.py
#           data/make_toy_dataset.py
#           data/README_data.md
#           data/CHECKSUMS.txt
#           data/README.md
#
#   4.  The two header-only toy CSVs (Files 10h–10i). These are tracked
#       despite the `data/toy_dataset/*.csv` ignore rule above, because
#       the two `!` exceptions in rule 8 un-ignore them by exact name:
#           data/toy_dataset/benign_sample.csv     (header row only)
#           data/toy_dataset/malicious_sample.csv  (header row only)
#
#   5.  The two directory placeholders (Files 10l–10m):
#           data/raw/.gitkeep
#           data/processed/.gitkeep
#
#   6.  Every `__init__.py` under `src/`, including the empty ones
#       (Files 11a, 11b, 12a, 13a, 14a, 15a, 16a). Python packaging
#       relies on these being present; a future maintainer must NOT add a
#       blanket `__init__.py` ignore rule.
#
#   7.  The source tree under `src/` (Files 11c–12h, 13b–13c, 14b–14e,
#       15b–15f, 16b–16d), the scripts under `scripts/` (Files 17a–19d),
#       the expected outputs under `expected_outputs/` (Files 20a–20j),
#       and the tests under `tests/` (Files 21a–21d). None of these
#       match any ignore rule.
#
# If `git status --ignored` after a clean clone shows any tracked file
# under `outputs/`, `data/raw/` (except `.gitkeep`), or
# `data/processed/` (except `.gitkeep`), one of the rules above has
# been mis-edited. See the "Relationship to .dockerignore" note below
# for the corresponding rules in the Docker build context.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# Relationship to .dockerignore
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#
# The recommended `.dockerignore` is documented in the header comment of
# the Dockerfile (File 6). It is a strict subset of this file: the Docker
# build context only needs the files that the runtime image actually
# uses, so the recommended `.dockerignore` repeats the rules for `.git/`,
# `.venv/`, `__pycache__/`, `outputs/`, `data/raw/`, `data/processed/`,
# and generated toy CSVs, but does NOT need to repeat IDE / OS / Jupyter
# rules (those files simply never enter a typical build context).
#
# If you add a new rule to either file, consider whether the same rule
# should be added to the other. As a rule of thumb:
#
#   • If the file is generated by Python tooling or by the artifact at
#     run time → add to both .gitignore and .dockerignore.
#   • If the file is local-only (IDE, OS, .env, local YAML overrides)
#     → add only to .gitignore.
#   • If the file is a committed input that the runtime needs (header-
#     only CSVs, CHECKSUMS.txt, __init__.py) → neither file.
#
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
# End of .gitignore
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
